17	
18	- Identifying the relative degree-six linear system with Proj(pi_*L) over an arbitrary base. [reason: Where h^0(L) jumps, pi_*L does not commute with base change and Proj(pi_*L) has the wrong fibres; the construction is valid only over the open locus of constant h^0. The base-independent model is the Abel-Jacobi incidence Sym^6(C/B) x_{Pic^6(C/B)} B, closed by separatedness of the relative Picard scheme, and it agrees with Proj(pi_*L) only over that open.]
19	- Deducing a global direct-sum splitting W ~= W_4 + W_5 from fibrewise balancedness F_s ~= O(4) + O(5). [reason: Fibrewise balancedness only supplies the relative Harder-Narasimhan filtration 0 -> W_4 -> W -> W_5 -> 0. A global splitting is an extra hypothesis: its obstruction is the extension class in Ext^1_{P^1_B}(L_4, L_5), which lies in H^1(B, gamma'_*O(1)) and need not vanish over a general base, while the fibrewise Ext vanishes so no local obstruction is visible.]
