22	- Deriving a local complete intersection or regular-immersion conclusion from a comparison between the actual codimension of a degeneracy locus and the generic determinantal expected codimension. [reason: Expected-codimension bookkeeping is neither necessary nor sufficient for regularity: a component of codimension below the determinantal expectation may still be locally cut out by a regular sequence of its actual codimension, and a nowhere-lci conclusion requires local ideal-depth or conormal calculations. The valid route is a closed immersion between schemes smooth over the base, whose codimension is the relative-dimension difference.]
23	- Treating the balanced reduced-support relative linear system as a closed pure-codimension-four subvariety of M_{6,6} and applying a Gysin morphism to the pair. [reason: The balanced reduced-support model is only an open piece of the evaluation-failure locus; Gysin for (M_{6,6}, Z) requires a closed (stratified) embedding obtained after h^0 and splitting-type stratification. Openness of balancedness inside the tetragonal locus supplies neither closedness nor purity.]
24	- Asserting that the balanced CE failure model maps monomorphically into an evaluation-failure locus in M_{6,6} while disclaiming the identification L ~= omega_C(-D). [reason: M_{6,6} forgets the pencil gamma, and a general genus-6 curve carries five distinct g^1_4's, so the underlying forgetful map is generically five-to-one; injectivity requires recovering gamma from L, whose only standard route is L ~= omega_C(-D), returning the pencil as |omega_C tensor L^{-1}|. Additionally, CE failure is a condition on the pair (C, gamma), not on a point of M_{6,6}, so the target of the claimed monomorphism is not well-defined without a stack-level full-faithfulness argument.]
