- `FND-0134` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}, 'generality': 'geometric fibres of a balanced genus-six degree-four CE family satisfying the weak relative-canonical normalization'}]: On a balanced fibre satisfying the weak CE normalization, h^0(L) = 3 (this lemma) combines with base-point-freeness of L on the balanced locus to make the general member of |L| ≅ P^2 consist of six DISTINCT points, hence the reduced-support open of the closed Abel–Jacobi incidence is nonempty and dense in every fibre of the P^2-bundle. Argument: if L had a base point p, then L(-p) would be a g^2_5 on a genus-six curve, forcing a smooth plane quintic, whose tetragonal pencil is projection from that point and is CE-unbalanced, hence excluded from the balanced locus; L base-point-free with h^0 = 3 gives a finite morphism φ_L : C → P^2 whose image spans, and in characteristic zero φ_L is separable onto its image, so a general line pulls back to a reduced divisor (Bertini), i.e. six distinct points.
- `FND-0135` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}, 'generality': 'smooth curves of genus 6 with a g^1_2 and a degree-4 map to P^1'}]: For a smooth genus-6 curve admitting maps h:C→P^1 of degree 2 and γ:C→P^1 of degree 4, Castelnuovo–Severi non-independence alone does not name the factorization; one must add that any common intermediate factor φ:C→C' has deg(φ) dividing 2 and at least 2, hence φ=h (up to automorphism of the target) and γ=q∘h with deg q=2.
- `FND-0136` [corroborated; {'generality': 'smooth genus-six curves with an effective degree-four pencil divisor', 'objects': ['L=ω_C(-D)', 'nonreduced locus in |L|']}]: If C has genus 6, D is an effective degree-four divisor with h^0(O(D))≥2, and L=ω_C(-D), then for every point p one has h^0(L(-2p))=h^0(O(D+2p))-1≥1. Hence every complete |L| contains a divisor nonreduced at p.
- `FND-0137` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: In C66-GEO-COMP, any inertia/stabilizer clause of the form 1→Aut(x_tilde)→Aut(x)→S_6 for a geometric point x of the Abel–Jacobi incidence I=Hilb⁶(C/B)×_{Pic⁶(C/B)}B must first name the stack and the moduli problem, because I is defined without any reference to γ. Over a scheme base every such Aut is trivial and the clause is vacuous; over a stack base the group depends on what the base classifies. Rigidity proofs that pass through 'the automorphism induces a PGL_2 transformation of P¹ fixing the marked images' prove the statement only for Aut(C,γ,Γ), automorphisms of the tetragonal cover preserving the divisor. They do not by themselves prove it for Aut(C,Γ), the group relevant to the forgetful map to M_{6,6}: an automorphism of C preserving Γ need not preserve the pencil, and triviality of the kernel is not formal, since a nontrivial automorphism of a genus-6 curve can fix as many as 2g+2=14 points and so can fix six marked points. Downstream artifacts must state which group they mean before citing such a clause.
- `FND-0139` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: Under the campaign's standing fibrewise-balanced hypothesis F_b≅O(4)⊕O(5), the jump stratum is empty: B_4=∅, so h⁰(L)≡3 and B=B_3. Proof: ATT-0066/CLM-0066-3 (dual-confirmed by REV-0110 and REV-0116) shows B_4 is contained in the locus of hyperelliptic fibres on which γ_b factors as q∘h through the hyperelliptic double cover; FND-0125 shows that for a hyperelliptic genus-6 curve with a degree-four pencil the relations bundle is exactly F=O(2)⊕O(7). These are incompatible with balancedness on every geometric fibre, so no fibre lies in B_4. Consequences: GAP-H0-1 as carried by ATT-0066 and ATT-0089 is closed under the balanced hypothesis; the k=4 branch of any P^{k-1}-bundle or dim(Y)+k−1 statement is vacuous; and the pencil recovery of FC-COMP-PENCIL-RECOVERY-AUT holds on all of B rather than only on a stratum.
- `FND-0141` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: Over a reduced locally Noetherian base carrying a balanced+(N) genus-6 family, the evaluation-failure scheme Z inside Conf_6(C/S) is S-smooth of pure relative dimension two (via the identification of Z with the S_6-torsor of orderings of the reduced-support open of P_S(pi_*L)), and the closed immersion Z->Conf_6 is therefore a regular immersion of pure codimension four with locally free conormal of rank four and a locally split conormal sequence. The lci conclusion is obtained from S-smoothness of both schemes, not from the excess of actual codimension 4 over the filtered determinantal expectation 6.
- `FND-0142` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}}]: Under (CE)+(BAL)+(N), every geometric fibre has h^0(O_C(D))=2, h^0(L)=3 and h^1(L)=2. The only Clifford-equality case that could produce h^0(D)=3 is a hyperelliptic fibre, and a hyperelliptic genus-6 degree-four cover cannot have F=O(4)+O(5). Constancy on the balanced locus therefore uses only exclusion of the hyperelliptic-composed pencil; it is not equivalent to exclusion of every non-primitive degree-four pencil through a positive-genus intermediate curve.
- `FND-0144` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}, 'objects': ['Z', 'U', 'V', 'H_V'], 'subproblem_id': 'C66-GEO-COMP'}]: For a finite étale S6-ordering torsor Z_V→V over a connected normal algebraic stack V, the scheme-theoretic component formula '[S6:H_V] irreducible components, each of degree |H_V|' is not valid without an additional representability/inertia-free hypothesis or a descent argument through a normal scheme atlas/coarse space controlling stack inertia. Stack inertia can act on the ordered fibre, so monodromy orbits alone do not establish irreducible components or stacky degrees.
- `FND-0146` [corroborated; Reusable technical principle for pushforward-vanishing arguments over nonreduced bases.]: Let d:K^0->K^1 be a morphism of finite locally free sheaves on a locally Noetherian base. If d tensor k(t) is injective for every point t, then the maximal minors generate the unit ideal and d is Zariski-locally a split injection; in particular ker(d)=0, with no reducedness hypothesis on the base.
- `FND-0147` [corroborated; C66-GEO-COMP; reusable for any downstream closure, purity, or intersection-theoretic step that needs the image of Theta as a locally regularly immersed constructible substack.]: On the balanced ordered model Z, the pencil (P,gamma) is functorially reconstructed from (C,Gamma) as the canonical morphism C -> P((p_*(omega_{C/T}(-Gamma)))^vee), with V = p_*(omega_{C/T}(-Gamma)) locally free of rank 2 commuting with arbitrary base change; consequently the forgetful morphism to M_{6,6} is a representable unramified quasi-finite monomorphism with inertia isomorphisms.
- `FND-0148` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}, 'characteristic': 0, 'objects': ['Z', 'U', 'S_6-ordering torsor', 'H_arith', 'H_geom'], 'subproblem_id': 'C66-GEO-COMP', 'hypotheses': ['U connected qcqs over a field k', 'Z -> U a finite etale right S_6-torsor', 'U normal integral locally Noetherian for Irr(Z) = pi_0(Z)', 'U_{k-bar} connected for H_geom normal in H_arith and the geometric component count']}]: Let U be a connected quasi-compact quasi-separated scheme over a field k, u a geometric point, and Z -> U a finite etale right S_6-torsor. The group H in Irr(Z) = H\S_6 for the k-scheme Z is the image H_arith of pi_1^{et}(U,u) -> S_6. If U is normal, integral, and locally Noetherian, connected components of Z are its irreducible components, indexed by left H_arith-cosets; each has degree |H_arith| over U and stabilizer conjugate to H_arith. If, in addition, U_{k-bar} is connected, then H_geom := image(pi_1^{et}(U_{k-bar},u) -> S_6) is normal in H_arith, [S_6:H_arith] <= [S_6:H_geom], and after base change Z has [S_6:H_geom] geometric irreducible components of degree |H_geom|. Thus irreducibility over k is weaker than geometric irreducibility. This identifies the group but does not compute either monodromy image for the balanced tetragonal family.
