applies (3.3) and (2.1) without constructing or citing the embedding, resolution, or (N). By contrast, CLM-0100-6 and CLM-0100-7 correctly leave balancedness of the bielliptic-composed and (1,2)-(2,2) complete-intersection examples conditional on (CE)+(N). Independently, Casnati–Ekedahl 1996 Theorem 2.1 supplies, for every finite flat Gorenstein cover of degree d≥3, the relative-canonical embedding induced by α^*E→ω_α and the Casnati–Ekedahl resolution; for d=4 this is a two-term resolution of I_C, and Canning–Larson arXiv:2208.02357v2 Lemma 10.6 records O_{P(E^∨)}(1)|_C ≅ ω_C⊗γ^*ω_{P^1}^∨. The genus-two composite is such a cover, so (CE) and the strong form of (N) (A trivial) hold for it by that general theorem if cited; this statement does not assert that the geometric example fails to be Gorenstein or that the involution or section counts are false. Those citations are absent from ATT-0100, so CLM-0100-12 remains unresolved as written. Future attempts may discharge (CE)+(N) for this example by citing Casnati–Ekedahl Theorem 2.1, but must still separately justify applying (3.3) to conclude balancedness.
- `FND-0151` [corroborated; {'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}, 'does_not_require': ['fibrewise balancedness', '(CE)', 'weak relative-canonical normalization (N)', 'computation of the monodromy image H'], 'generality': 'any group G with subgroup H; applied to any finite etale right G-torsor over a connected base', 'hypotheses': ['G a group and H a subgroup', 'Z -> U a finite etale right G-torsor', 'U connected, so that pi_0(Z) is the monodromy-orbit set H\\G', 'U normal and locally Noetherian to identify Irr(Z) with pi_0(Z)'], 'objects': ['finite etale right G-torsor Z -> U', 'orbit set H\\G of left multiplication', 'right cosets Hg', 'right deck action', 'component stabilizer g^{-1}Hg'], 'subproblem_id': 'C66-GEO-COMP'}]: Let G be a group and H a subgroup of G. The orbits of the left multiplication action of H on G are the sets Hg={hg : h in H}. Under standard elementary nomenclature these are the right cosets of H, and the set of such orbits is denoted H\G; the left cosets are the sets gH, and the set of left cosets is denoted G/H. The phrase 'left cosets H\S_6' in ATT-0101's theorem statement and the phrase 'left H_arith-cosets' in FND-0148 are therefore elementary mislabels of the object Hg, even though the notation H\S_6 itself is the standard symbol for that object. Now let U be a connected scheme, let Z->U be a finite etale right G-torsor, and let H be the image of etale monodromy acting by left multiplication on a geometric fibre after a choice of trivialization. The connected components of Z are indexed by the orbit set H\G={Hg}. The right deck action of G is well-defined on this set by Hg . g'=Hgg', is transitive, and the stabilizer of Hg is exactly g^{-1}Hg. Right multiplication on the genuine left-coset set G/H={gH} is well-defined if and only if H is normal in G; in particular it is well-defined when H is {1}, A_6, or S_6 inside S_6, and it fails for every non-normal monodromy image. Any artifact that writes gH and then applies the right deck action, or that computes the stabilizer of the component indexed by Hg as gHg^{-1}, is ill-typed unless H is normal, in which case both conjugates equal H. Identifying these connected components with irreducible components additionally requires U to be normal and locally Noetherian. This constraint does not refute the component, degree, dimension, or transitivity clauses of ATT-0101, which already index components by H_alpha g and compute the stabilizer as g^{-1}H_alpha g. Downstream C66-GEO-COMP and C66-TATE-* artifacts must write 'the orbit set H\G of left multiplication' (equivalently, 'the set of right cosets Hg') and must record the component stabilizer as g^{-1}Hg.
- `FND-0152` [corroborated; {'base': 'normal finite-type schemes over an algebraically closed characteristic-zero field', 'case': {'g': 6, 'n': 6}, 'coefficients': 'Q', 'does_not_assert': ['Tate type of H^*(Z_alpha^1) or H^*(Z_alpha)', 'computation of the monodromy image H_alpha', 'a component or degree formula over an algebraic stack with inertia', 'integral coefficients or coarse-moduli descent'], 'hypotheses': ['confirmed ATT-0101 component classification CLM-0101-3 and CLM-0101-4 over a normal base', 'scheme-level, no stack inertia', 'Z_alpha -> U_alpha a finite etale right S_6-torsor', 'U_alpha normal integral locally Noetherian, so Irr(Z_alpha)=pi_0(Z_alpha)', 'components indexed by the orbit set H_alpha\\S_6 of left multiplication (right cosets H_alpha g), with stabilizer of Z_alpha^1 equal to H_alpha', 'Q-coefficients, so that |H_alpha| and |S_6| are invertible', 'additive S_6-equivariant functor to Q-vector spaces or mixed Hodge structures, including Betti H^*(-;Q) and H^{BM}_*(-;Q)'], 'objects': ['Z_alpha', 'U_alpha', 'H_alpha', 'Z_alpha^1', 'H^*(Z_alpha;Q)', 'H^{BM}_*(Z_alpha;Q)'], 'subproblem_id': 'C66-GEO-COMP'}]: Let k be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and let S be a normal finite-type k-scheme carrying a balanced+(CE)+(N) genus-six family as in ATT-0101. For each connected component S_alpha of S, let U_alpha be the reduced-support open of the Abel-Jacobi P^2-bundle over S_alpha and let Z_alpha -> U_alpha be the finite etale right S_6-torsor Ord(U_alpha). Let H_alpha be the image of etale monodromy pi_1^{et}(U_alpha) -> S_6, acting by left multiplication on a trivialized geometric fibre. The statement is scheme-level: it assumes the confirmed ATT-0101 classification (CLM-0101-3, CLM-0101-4) and does not extend to algebraic stacks with inertia (FND-0144). Then the connected components of Z_alpha coincide with its irreducible components and are indexed by the orbit set H_alpha\S_6 of left multiplication (the right cosets H_alpha g). The right deck action is transitive on this set, and the stabilizer of the identity-coset component Z_alpha^1 is exactly H_alpha. Right multiplication by any element of S_6 is a U_alpha-automorphism of Z_alpha, so all components are isomorphic as U_alpha-schemes. The residual H_alpha-action on Z_alpha^1 is free and simply transitive on geometric fibres; Z_alpha^1 -> U_alpha is therefore a connected finite etale cover of degree |H_alpha|, hence a Galois cover with group H_alpha. For any additive functor F from finite-type k-schemes (or their Betti analytifications) to Q-vector spaces or to mixed Hodge structures over Q that is functorial for the right S_6-action — in particular ordinary Betti cohomology H^*(-;Q) and Borel-Moore homology H^{BM}_*(-;Q), each with its mixed Hodge structure — there is a canonical isomorphism of S_6-objects F(Z_alpha) isomorphic to Ind_{H_alpha}^{S_6} F(Z_alpha^1), where induction is Q[S_6] tensor_{Q[H_alpha]} (-) and the H_alpha-action on F(Z_alpha^1) is the residual deck action. Because |H_alpha| is invertible in Q, the finite-covering transfer identifies F(U_alpha) with F(Z_alpha^1)^{H_alpha}; Frobenius reciprocity then gives F(Z_alpha)^{S_6} isomorphic to F(Z_alpha^1)^{H_alpha} isomorphic to F(U_alpha). Two consequences, with the hypotheses above retained. (i) The S_6-isotypic decomposition of F(Z_alpha) is determined by the pair consisting of the subgroup H_alpha and the H_alpha-object F(Z_alpha^1); the component count [S_6:H_alpha] is not independent data. Computing H_alpha (GAP-COMP-MONODROMY) determines the induction functor but does not compute F(Z_alpha^1) as an H_alpha-object, which remains a separate geometric and Hodge input (GAP-TATE). (ii) The isomorphism is purely formal and upgrades nothing to Tate type: it is compatible with F(Z_alpha^1) carrying non-Tate summands (FND-0053). It is stated with Q-coefficients on schemes, so it carries none of the integral-coefficient or coarse-space content excluded by FND-0075.

